{"task_id": "HumanEval/133", "solution": "import math\n\ndef sum_squares(lst):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of squared numbers in the given list,\n    after rounding each number up to the nearest integer.\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum_of_squares = 0\n    for number in lst:\n        # Round each element to the upper integer (Ceiling)\n        ceiled_number = math.ceil(number)\n        # Square the ceilinged number and add to the total\n        total_sum_of_squares += int(ceiled_number * ceiled_number)\n    return total_sum_of_squares"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/134", "solution": "def check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(txt):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the last character of a string is an alphabetical, single-letter token.\n    Returns False for empty strings or strings ending with whitespace.\n    \"\"\"\n    # FR1: Return False for empty input strings.\n    if not txt:\n        return False\n\n    # FR2: Return False if the final character is a whitespace character.\n    if txt[-1].isspace():\n        return False\n\n    # Find the last token by splitting the string from the right at the last space.\n    # If no space is found, parts will contain only the original string.\n    parts = txt.rsplit(' ', 1)\n    last_token = parts[-1]\n\n    # FR3 & FR4: Check if the last token is exactly one alphabetical character.\n    # This covers both length and character type.\n    return len(last_token) == 1 and last_token.isalpha()"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "```python\ndef is_nested(string):\n    '''\n    Checks if there's a subsequence of the form \"[[]]\" (two opening brackets followed by two closing brackets).\n    This is equivalent to finding four indices i < j < k < l such that s[i]=='[', s[j]=='[', s[k]==']', s[l]==']'.\n    '''\n    n = len(string)\n    if n < 4:  # Minimum length for \"[[]]\" subsequence\n        return False\n\n    # suffix_close[x] stores the count of ']' characters from index x to n-1\n    suffix_close = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        suffix_close[i] = suffix_close[i+1] + (1 if string[i] == ']' else 0)\n\n    open_count = 0  # Counts '[' characters encountered from the beginning up to the current position (exclusive)\n    for j, char in enumerate(string):\n        if char == '[':\n            # If we have at least one '[' before the current 'j' (s[i])\n            # AND at least two ']' after the current 'j' (s[k], s[l])\n            # then we've found a nested subsequence \"[[]]\"\n            if open_count >= 1 and suffix_close[j+1] >= 2:\n                return True\n            open_count += 1\n    \n    return False\n```"}
